
FRAUDX - WEBSITE PROTECTION
THREAT RESEARCH NOTES

PURPOSE

This document describes the threat categories that the Website
Protection Module is designed to explain when the scanner reports
relevant evidence.

1. PHISHING
A website may imitate a legitimate service to trick visitors into
revealing personal or login information.

2. BRAND IMPERSONATION
A website may copy the name, logo or appearance of a trusted brand
to appear genuine.

3. CREDENTIAL THEFT
A suspicious website may attempt to collect usernames, passwords
or other account login details.

4. MALWARE
A website may distribute harmful files or attempt to make visitors
download malicious software.

5. SUSPICIOUS REDIRECT
A website may redirect visitors to unexpected or potentially
dangerous destinations.

6. FAKE PAYMENT PAGE
A fraudulent payment page may attempt to collect card or banking
information.

7. OTP HARVESTING
A fraudulent page may ask visitors to enter one-time passwords
that could be used to authorize transactions or access accounts.

8. FAKE GOVERNMENT PORTAL
A fraudulent website may imitate a government website to collect
personal information or payments.

9. INSECURE CONNECTION
An insecure connection can expose information to interception.
HTTPS alone does not prove that a website is trustworthy.

10. UNKNOWN THREAT
Used when the scanner reports an indicator that does not match
a supported threat category.

RESEARCH AND IMPLEMENTATION RULE

The module must explain only the evidence actually supplied by the
scanner. It must not invent threat detections or claim that a
website is malicious without supporting evidence.

PRECAUTION PRINCIPLE

Advice should be appropriate to the detected threat and to the
actions already taken by the user.
